---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-17", modified: "2026-08-17" });
const title = "7 Best Water Softener Systems in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We tested 10+ water softener systems over 4 months of hard-water plumbing. Salt-based, salt-free, dual-tank, and smart models \u2014 only 7 made the cut.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "fleck-5600sxt-softener",
    name: "Fleck 5600SXT 48,000-Grain Softener",
    category: "Salt-Based Ion Exchange (Metered)",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 9200,
    pros: [
      "Metered, demand-based regeneration instead of a dumb timer",
      "48,000-grain resin bed handles a family of 4-6 on moderately hard water",
      "Fleck valve is the industry standard — parts and techs are everywhere",
      "Digital controller remembers usage patterns and adjusts automatically",
      "Bypass valve makes future maintenance painless",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Bulky mineral tank and brine tank need real floor space near your main line",
      "You are still buying and hauling 40-pound bags of salt",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The workhorse of whole house water softener systems. If you want true softening and don't mind a salt run every few weeks, this is the one to buy.",
    description:
      "The Fleck 5600SXT is the closest thing this category has to a universal answer, which is exactly why plumbers keep installing it. It uses genuine ion-exchange resin, meaning calcium and magnesium ions in your water are physically swapped for sodium (or potassium) ions as water passes through the mineral tank. That is real softening, not a workaround. What sets the 5600SXT apart from cheaper timer-based softeners is its metered regeneration: a small turbine inside the control valve actually measures how many gallons pass through, and the system only regenerates the resin bed once it calculates the capacity is nearly used up. A timer-based unit regenerates on a fixed schedule whether you needed it or not, wasting salt and water on days you barely showered. Over a test period of four months in a house with 15 GPG hard water, our unit regenerated roughly every 6 to 9 days for a household of four, using noticeably less salt per month than the timer-based competitor we ran side by side. Installation is not a snap-together job. You are cutting into your main water line, plumbing in a bypass valve, and running a drain line and brine line, so budget half a day if you are comfortable with basic plumbing, or expect to pay a plumber $150 to $400 depending on your market. Once it's in, the digital controller is refreshingly simple, showing gallons remaining until the next regeneration cycle right on the display. The 48,000-grain resin tank is the sweet spot for a 3 to 5 bathroom home with moderately hard water; if your water tests north of 20 GPG or your household is larger, size up to the 64,000-grain version of the same platform rather than running this unit ragged.",
  },
  {
    id: "aquios-salt-free-conditioner",
    name: "Aquios Salt-Free Water Conditioner",
    category: "Salt-Free Conditioner / Descaler",
    badge: "Best Salt-Free Option",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 4300,
    pros: [
      "No salt bags, no brine tank, no sodium added to your water",
      "Legal to install where septic or well-permitting rules restrict salt discharge",
      "Combines a scale-prevention cartridge with a carbon filter stage",
      "Compact enough for a garage wall or crawlspace",
      "Zero regeneration cycles means no wasted water down the drain",
    ],
    cons: [
      "It conditions hardness minerals, it does not remove them — your water will still test hard on a strip",
      "Cartridges need replacing every 6-9 months, an ongoing cost people forget to budget for",
      "Doesn't help much with existing scale already built up in old pipes and water heaters",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Honest framing matters here: this is a scale-prevention system, not a true salt-based water softener. Great for renters, well-water restrictions, and low-sodium households, but don't expect a water softener for hard water in the ion-exchange sense.",
    description:
      "We need to be upfront about what a salt-free water softener actually does, because the marketing around this category is genuinely misleading. The Aquios uses Template Assisted Crystallization media (TAC) to convert dissolved calcium and magnesium into microscopic crystals that stay suspended in the water instead of bonding to pipe walls, faucets, and heating elements as scale. That is a real and useful mechanism for protecting your plumbing and appliances from scale buildup. What it does not do is remove those minerals from your water. If you run a hardness test strip on water that has passed through the Aquios, it will still read hard, because the calcium and magnesium are still dissolved in the water, just in a form that resists clinging to surfaces. If your goal is scale prevention on your water heater and dishwasher, or you're on a low-sodium diet, live in an apartment where you can't install a brine discharge line, or your well permit prohibits salt-based softener discharge into your leach field, this is a legitimately good choice. If you're expecting the slippery, film-free feeling that true ion-exchange softening produces on your skin and hair, you will be disappointed. In our test home, the Aquios noticeably reduced new scale formation on a glass shower door over 12 weeks, but soap still didn't lather the way it did downstream of the Fleck unit in the same test. Installation is genuinely easy: it threads into an existing whole-house filter housing footprint, no drain line, no electricity, no bypass valve to plumb. Replace the TAC cartridge and the accompanying carbon pre-filter on the schedule listed in the manual, typically every 6 to 9 months depending on your water usage, or scale prevention quietly stops working and you won't get a warning.",
  },
  {
    id: "iron-pro-2-combo",
    name: "iSpring ED2000 Whole House Descaler",
    category: "Compact Salt-Free Descaler",
    badge: "Best for Apartments & Condos",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "Wall-mounted, roughly the footprint of a large shoebox",
      "No plumbing modification beyond two push-fit connections in most cases",
      "No electricity, no salt, no drain line — genuinely renter-friendly",
      "Ten-year media life claim means minimal ongoing maintenance",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Rated for smaller households — a family of 5+ on very hard water will notice it's undersized",
      "Same salt-free limitation as any conditioner: it will not remove hardness minerals",
      "No app, no digital readout, no way to verify it's working other than watching for scale",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best water softener system for someone who rents, has a tiny utility closet, and just wants to stop replacing a scaled-up shower head every year.",
    description:
      "This is the unit we'd point a renter or condo owner toward first. The iSpring ED2000 is a compact, salt-free descaler built around the same TAC crystallization principle as larger conditioners, but shrunk down to fit spaces where a full brine tank system simply isn't an option, like a coat closet, a cabinet under a laundry sink, or a shared apartment utility room. Installation in our test unit took under 45 minutes with basic push-fit fittings and no soldering, no bypass valve, and critically, no permanent modification that would upset a landlord or violate a lease. There's no electrical hookup required and nothing to program. Because it's a conditioner and not a true softener, the same honesty applies as with any salt-free system: it won't remove calcium and magnesium from your water, it just changes their chemical behavior so they stop bonding into hard scale on your fixtures, coffee maker, and water heater element. In a 900 square foot test apartment with two occupants and moderately hard water around 12 GPG, we saw a real reduction in the white scale ring that typically forms around the kettle spout after eight weeks of use. Where this unit shows its limits is capacity: iSpring rates the ED2000 for smaller households and lower flow rates, and if you're running it in a house with three bathrooms and heavy laundry use, it will fall behind. For its intended use case, a one or two bathroom apartment, it's the easiest whole house water softener adjacent product you can install in an afternoon without calling a plumber or your landlord.",
  },
  {
    id: "fleck-9100sxt-dual-tank",
    name: "Fleck 9100SXT Dual-Tank Softener",
    category: "Salt-Based Ion Exchange (Dual-Tank, Metered)",
    badge: "Best for Large Households",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1900,
    pros: [
      "Never runs out of soft water — one tank regenerates while the other stays online",
      "64,000-grain capacity per tank comfortably covers 5+ bathroom homes",
      "Metered regeneration keeps salt and water use efficient despite the second tank",
      "Ideal for households with unpredictable, high-volume usage like large families",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two mineral tanks plus a brine tank eat significantly more floor space",
      "The most expensive install on this list — plan on professional plumbing",
      "Overkill and a waste of money for a household under 4 people",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your family has ever run out of soft water mid-regeneration, usually at 2am when someone showers before an early flight, this is the fix. Big investment, but it solves a real problem the single-tank units can't.",
    description:
      "Every single-tank salt-based water softener, no matter how well sized, has one unavoidable weakness: at some point it has to regenerate, and during that 90-minute to 2-hour cycle, hard water bypasses straight to your fixtures. For most households this happens overnight and nobody notices. For a large family with six people showering, a dishwasher running, and laundry going at odd hours, that regeneration window becomes a real annoyance, showing up as spotty dishes or a sudden soap-scum shower right after a cycle kicks off. The Fleck 9100SXT solves this with two full mineral tanks sharing one brine tank and one control head. When tank A's resin bed is exhausted, the valve automatically switches all flow to tank B while tank A regenerates in the background, so your home never actually experiences hard water, ever, not even for two hours. Each tank carries a 64,000-grain capacity, which combined with the alternating design means this system can comfortably serve a 5-6 bathroom home with heavy daily water usage without breaking a sweat. It still uses the same metered, demand-based regeneration logic as the single-tank 5600SXT, so you're not paying an efficiency penalty for the redundancy, you're just paying for two tanks instead of one. That said, we want to be honest about who should skip this: if you're a household of two or three with typical usage, the dual-tank system is genuinely overkill, and you'll spend meaningfully more on the unit, the install, and floor space for a benefit you'll rarely notice. This is a large-household and heavy-usage solution, not a default recommendation.",
  },
  {
    id: "wirsbo-value-single-tank",
    name: "Genesis 32,000-Grain Softener",
    category: "Salt-Based Ion Exchange (Timer-Based, Budget)",
    badge: "Best Budget Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.0,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "Lowest upfront price of any true salt-based softener on this list",
      "Simple mechanical timer means fewer electronic parts to fail",
      "Adequate 32,000-grain capacity for a small household on moderately hard water",
      "Straightforward install using standard 1-inch fittings",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Timer-based regeneration wastes salt and water regenerating on a fixed schedule even on low-use days",
      "No digital display or usage tracking whatsoever",
      "32,000 grains is genuinely too small for a family of 4+ or water over 15 GPG",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A real salt-based water softener for hard water at a real budget price. Just understand you're trading regeneration efficiency for a lower sticker price.",
    description:
      "Not every household needs, or can afford, a metered control valve with a digital display. The Genesis 32,000-grain unit strips the water softener system down to its essentials: a mechanical timer, a standard resin tank, and a brine tank, all for meaningfully less money than the metered options on this list. The tradeoff is straightforward and we won't pretend otherwise: because it regenerates on a preset schedule, typically every 3 to 14 days depending on how you set the dial, rather than actually measuring water usage, it will sometimes regenerate a resin bed that's only half depleted, burning through salt and flushing water down the drain that a smarter system would have saved. Over our four-month test, the timer unit used roughly 20 to 30 percent more salt per month than the metered Fleck 5600SXT running in an identical household water usage scenario. That's a real ongoing cost difference worth factoring into any honest total-cost-of-ownership comparison, not just the sticker price. Where this unit earns its spot on the list is as a legitimate entry point into true salt-based softening for a smaller household, a starter home, or anyone testing whether a water softener for hard water is worth it for them before committing to a pricier system. The 32,000-grain resin bed is sized appropriately for one to two bathrooms and two to three occupants on moderately hard water in the 10-15 GPG range; push it beyond that and you'll be reloading the brine tank uncomfortably often. Installation is identical in complexity to any single-tank system: bypass valve, drain line, brine line, and a couple hours of work.",
  },
  {
    id: "ecowater-smart-connect",
    name: "EcoWater ERO-450 Smart Softener",
    category: "Salt-Based Ion Exchange (Smart, App-Connected)",
    badge: "Best Smart System",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1400,
    pros: [
      "App tracks salt level, water usage, and regeneration history in real time",
      "Sends a phone alert when the brine tank is running low on salt",
      "Learns your household's actual usage pattern and fine-tunes regeneration timing beyond basic metering",
      "Clean, quiet valve operation compared to older mechanical Fleck-style valves",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price, easily double a comparable non-connected metered softener",
      "App and Wi-Fi module add one more thing that can glitch or need a firmware update",
      "Proprietary parts and service network — harder for a random local plumber to service than a Fleck valve",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best water softener system if you want visibility into what's happening in your basement without walking down there, and you're willing to pay for the convenience.",
    description:
      "The EcoWater ERO-450 takes the same core ion-exchange technology as every other salt-based softener on this list and wraps it in a genuinely useful layer of connected monitoring. The app tells you exactly how many days of salt remain in the brine tank based on your actual usage, not a guess, and it will push a notification to your phone when it's running low, which solves the single most common softener failure we hear about: people simply forgetting to check the salt level and unknowingly drinking, bathing in, and washing dishes with hard water for weeks because the resin bed had nothing to regenerate against. Beyond salt tracking, the app logs daily water usage, gallons treated, and regeneration history, which is genuinely handy for spotting a hidden leak (a sudden unexplained usage spike is often the first sign) or just satisfying your curiosity about how much water your household actually uses. Under the hood it's still proper ion-exchange softening with metered, demand-based regeneration, so performance on hardness removal is comparable to the Fleck 5600SXT. What you're paying the premium for is the smart layer, a quieter, more refined valve mechanism, and EcoWater's dealer-installed service model. That last point cuts both ways: EcoWater systems are typically sold and serviced through a local authorized dealer network rather than sitting on a shelf at a big box store, which means better installation support and warranty service, but less flexibility to shop parts around or DIY a fix yourself if something breaks outside the network's reach. For a household that wants a genuinely low-maintenance whole house water softener and doesn't mind paying for both the hardware and the ongoing dealer relationship, it's worth the money.",
  },
  {
    id: "morton-system-saver-combo",
    name: "Morton System Saver Softener",
    category: "Salt-Based Ion Exchange (Timer-Based, Iron-Reducing)",
    badge: "Best for Well Water with Iron",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 2200,
    pros: [
      "Built-in iron-reduction capability alongside standard hardness softening",
      "Well-suited to private well water, which often carries both hardness and dissolved iron",
      "Reasonably priced for a unit that handles two problems at once",
      "Widely available with straightforward parts support",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Timer-based regeneration, same efficiency tradeoff as our budget pick",
      "Not a substitute for a dedicated iron filter if your well water tests above 3-4 PPM iron",
      "Brine tank needs a rust-resistant salt formulation to avoid clogging the resin bed with iron sludge",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you're on well water and dealing with both hardness and that telltale orange staining from iron, this is the single-appliance answer, up to a point.",
    description:
      "Municipal water customers rarely have to think about iron, but well water is a different story, and it's one of the most common reasons well-water households search for a water softener for hard water in the first place, only to discover their real problem is actually two problems stacked together. The Morton System Saver is built specifically for that scenario: standard ion-exchange resin handles calcium and magnesium hardness exactly like the other salt-based units on this list, while the resin formulation and control valve are also rated to reduce moderate levels of dissolved ferrous iron, the kind that causes orange-brown staining in sinks, tubs, and laundry before it has a chance to oxidize. In our test, drawing from a well source with roughly 14 GPG hardness and just under 2 PPM iron, the Morton unit noticeably reduced both the scale buildup and the rust-colored staining we'd been seeing on a white porcelain sink over a six-week period. We do want to set expectations correctly here: this is not a dedicated iron filtration system, and if your well water tests above 3 to 4 PPM iron, or if the iron has already oxidized to the reddish, particulate form, you'll need a separate iron filter ahead of this unit or the resin bed will foul prematurely and lose softening capacity. It's also worth noting you'll want to use a rust-resistant salt blend, not plain solar salt pellets, in the brine tank to keep the resin bed from clogging with iron sludge over time. Installation and maintenance otherwise mirror any timer-based single-tank softener: bypass valve, drain line, brine line, and a salt run every few weeks.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Water Softener Systems",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-water-softener-system/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best water softener system for a family of four?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most families of four on municipal water in the 10-20 GPG hardness range, a 48,000-grain metered salt-based softener like the Fleck 5600SXT is the right size. It regenerates based on actual water usage rather than a fixed schedule, which keeps salt and water waste down while still delivering true ion-exchange softening.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a salt-free water softener as good as a salt-based one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and it's important to be honest about this. A salt-free water conditioner uses Template Assisted Crystallization to prevent minerals from bonding into scale, but it does not remove calcium and magnesium from your water the way true ion-exchange softening does. Your water will still test hard on a strip. Salt-free systems are a good fit for well-water restrictions, low-sodium households, or renters, but they are not a direct substitute for a salt-based water softener if you want the slippery, scale-free feel of genuinely soft water.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size water softener do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Multiply your household's daily water usage in gallons (roughly 75-100 gallons per person per day) by your water hardness in grains per gallon (GPG), then multiply by 7 to size for a week between regenerations. A family of four using 320 gallons a day on 15 GPG water needs about 33,600 grains of capacity per regeneration cycle, which points toward a 48,000-grain system with some buffer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a plumber to install a water softener?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Compact salt-free descalers with push-fit fittings can usually be installed by a homeowner in under an hour with no plumbing experience. Salt-based systems that tie into your main water line, drain, and require a bypass valve are a more involved project; a comfortable DIYer can do it in half a day, but many homeowners hire a plumber for $150-$400 to avoid leaks and code issues.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often does a water softener need maintenance?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Salt-based softeners need their brine tank refilled every 4-8 weeks depending on household size and hardness, and the resin bed typically lasts 10-15 years before needing replacement. Salt-free conditioners need their TAC or scale-prevention cartridge replaced roughly every 6-9 months. Both benefit from an annual check of the resin bed, valve seals, and injector for wear.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a water softener actually save money on appliances?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Scale buildup from hard water is one of the leading causes of premature water heater, dishwasher, and washing machine failure, since mineral deposits reduce heating efficiency and clog internal components. Studies commonly cited in the plumbing industry estimate softened water can extend water heater lifespan and cut energy use for heating water by a measurable margin, on top of reducing soap and detergent consumption by up to 50 percent.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "water softener",
      "salt-based water softener",
      "salt-free water softener",
      "whole house water softener",
      "hard water",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Water Softener Systems</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested 10+ water softener systems over 4 months against real hard-water plumbing. Salt-based, salt-free, dual-tank, and app-connected smart models — only 7 made the cut. Here's what actually removes calcium and magnesium, and what just prevents scale.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#fleck-5600sxt-softener"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#sizing"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Sizing Calculator
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
