        <!-- SECTION 3: Salt vs Salt-Free -->
        <div id="salt-vs-saltfree" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Salt-Based vs. Salt-Free: What's the Real Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most confused decision in the entire category, and it's the one we get the most questions about. Marketing copy for salt-free systems frequently uses the word "softener" right alongside "no salt required," implying you can get the same result as a salt-based water softener without the hassle of buying and hauling salt bags. That framing is, honestly, a bit of a stretch, and it sets buyers up to feel misled once the product arrives home.
          </p>

          <p>
            A true <strong>salt-based water softener</strong> performs ion exchange: it physically removes calcium and magnesium ions from your water and replaces them with sodium ions. The mineral content of your water genuinely changes. That's why softened water feels slippery on your skin (there's no calcium left for soap to bind with, forming a rinse-resistant film) and why a hardness test strip on softened water reads at or near zero. It's also why salt-based systems need a brine tank, a drain line for the wastewater flushed out during regeneration, and periodic salt refills, real ongoing maintenance in exchange for real results.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>salt-free water softener</strong>, more accurately called a conditioner or descaler, does not remove anything from your water. Using Template Assisted Crystallization media, it converts dissolved calcium and magnesium into a crystalline form that no longer readily bonds to surfaces, meaning existing scale problems on your fixtures and appliances slow down or stop getting worse. But the minerals are still dissolved in the water leaving your tap. A hardness test strip will still read hard. Soap will still be somewhat less sudsy than it would be with true soft water. Your skin won't get that distinctive slippery, soft feeling salt-based softening produces.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Honest Bottom Line on Salt-Free</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If your goal is protecting your water heater, dishwasher, and pipes from new scale buildup, a salt-free conditioner is a legitimate, lower-maintenance tool that genuinely works for that specific job. If your goal is the classic soft-water experience, silky skin, sudsy soap, spot-free glassware straight out of the dishwasher, only a true salt-based ion-exchange system delivers it. Buying a salt-free unit expecting the second outcome is the most common source of disappointed reviews we found researching this category.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            So who should actually choose salt-free? Renters and condo owners who can't modify plumbing for a drain line or brine discharge. Households on a low-sodium diet where even the modest sodium increase from ion exchange is a genuine concern (though it's worth noting a potassium chloride alternative exists for salt-based systems if this is your only objection). And anyone on a private well where local septic or leach-field permitting restricts salt-based softener discharge, which is a real and increasingly common regulatory constraint in some counties. Everyone else who wants the full soft-water experience should budget for a true salt-based whole house water softener.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Sizing -->
        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: How Many Grains Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water softeners are sized in <strong>grains of hardness removal capacity</strong> between regenerations, and getting this number right matters more than any brand decision on this list. An undersized system regenerates constantly, burning through salt and water and shortening the resin bed's life. An oversized system is simply wasted money sitting in your basement. The math isn't complicated, but almost nobody does it before buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Know Your Water Hardness in GPG</h3>
          <p>
            Hardness is measured in <strong>grains per gallon (GPG)</strong>. If your home is on municipal water, your local water utility publishes an annual water quality report with this number, or you can call and ask directly. Well water owners need an independent test, either a mail-in lab kit or a simple in-home test strip, since well hardness varies significantly even between neighboring properties. As a general reference: 1-3.5 GPG is considered slightly hard, 3.5-7 GPG is moderately hard, 7-10.5 GPG is hard, and anything above 10.5 GPG is considered very hard water. Much of the central and western United States commonly tests between 15 and 25 GPG, well into the "very hard" range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Know Your Household's Daily Water Usage</h3>
          <p>
            The commonly cited industry estimate is 75-100 gallons per person, per day, covering showers, laundry, dishwashing, and general use. A household of four at 80 gallons per person lands around 320 gallons a day; a household of two lands closer to 160 gallons a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Do the Grain Capacity Math</h3>
          <p>
            Multiply daily gallons used by GPG hardness to get your daily grain removal requirement, then multiply by the number of days you want between regenerations, typically 7, to size the system with a comfortable buffer.
          </p>

